The 1-RTT keys a packet from the peer is opened with, for reading.

Nothing here changes what the library does. It lets the client open the node's next packets ahead
of ngtcp2_conn_read_pkt — Salamander off, header protection off, the AEAD opened — on other cores,
with the key, IV and packet number the library will itself use, which it could not see before:
the key's IV and the largest packet number received live inside the connection. The decrypt and
hp_mask callbacks then answer from what was computed when, and only when, every input they are
called with is the one the computation had (src/quic/rxopen.c); otherwise they compute as always.
So what the library decides, and everything on the wire, is unchanged.

quic-go opens a packet in the connection's own goroutine (vendor/quic-go/connection.go, handlePacket
→ unpacker.UnpackShortHeader), so this is not the reference's arrangement: it is the maintainer's
decision of 2026-09-29, to use the cores an MT7621 leaves idle while one of them holds a Hysteria2
download (CONCURRENCY.md).

